                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "ck13":</h3><p><b>CK-13</b> is the name of a microphone capsule developed by Telefunken Elektroakustik (formerly Telefunken USA). </p><p><img src="/images/mic_extras/telefunkenusa/CK13.jpg" width="120" alt="CK13 Capsule" class="thumb shaft" />It is a large-diaphragm, edge- terminated, multipattern capable condenser capsule modeled after <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/ck12">AKG&#8217;s CK-12</a>.</p><p>Telefunken Elektroakustik began developing a historically-accurate CK-12 reproduction capsule in 2004-2005. The CK-13 differs from the company&#8217;s CK-12 reissue in just one respect: the diaphragms, rather than gold-coated Mylar, are made of a titanium-aluminum alloy called Alloy 2213&trade;.</p><blockquote><p>Alloy 2213 &#8230; is far more rigid than gold&#8230; [The capsule] provides greater accuracy in the low frequency ranges of the audio spectrum by attaining a higher damping factor to the motion of the membrane.</p><p>Alloy 2213 is lighter than gold, and when combined with the edge terminated CK-12 system provides an &#8220;airy&#8221; and &#8220;delicate&#8221; representation of the upper ranges of the audio spectrum. This affords the user a higher degree of detail&#8230;</p><p>[This capsule] can better withstand moisture, smoke, and extreme volume, thus requiring less maintenance over the years, and provides the user a superior capture of the sound to be recorded.</p></blockquote><p></p>
